  "title": "Separation through the decoder's kernel",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "For the binary left-pigeon principle LP_n (n = 2^l holes, l >= 2), if B >= 2, k <= B and 2lB - 1 <= n, then every row-linear polynomial p of degree at most k derived from LP_n in polynomial-calculus degree B lies in the kernel K_k = {p : tau~(p) in Ideal_k(F^fun_{n+1,n})} of the reduced decoder (pigeon bit f_x,t to sum_{z: z_t = 1} X_x,z, hole bit g_h,j to sum_y y_j X_y,h) modulo Braun's unary degree-k ideal. So the dynamic separation question for LP_n is a static one: every subspace of L_k meeting K_k only in zero is separated. The (l + 1)^2 inverse-counting identities kappa_{a,j} = sum_h a(h) g_h,j + sum_x x_j a(f_x) lie in K_1 for every n.",
